  },
  {
    "index": 19,
    "sentence": "Let $mn-1$ divide $(n^2-n+1)^2$.",
    "sentence-type": "answer",
    "sentence-category-reason": "Restates the divisibility assumption again.",
    "sentence-category": "Read"
  },
  {
    "index": 20,
    "sentence": "Then $(mn-1)$ divides $m^2(n^2-n+1)^2 = (mn)^4 - 2m^2n^3 + 3m^2n^2 - 2m^2n + m^2$.",
    "sentence-type": "answer",
    "sentence-category-reason": "Multiplies by m^2 and expands to obtain a new divisibility statement.",
    "sentence-category": "Implement"
  },
  {
    "index": 21,
    "sentence": "Since $mn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{mn-1}$, we have $1^4 - 2m^2(1/m) + 3m^2 - 2m^2(1/m) + m^2 = 1 - 2m + 3m^2 - 2m + m^2 = 4m^2 - 4m + 1 = (2m-1)^2$.",
    "sentence-type": "answer",
    "sentence-category-reason": "Performs a concrete modular substitution and algebraic simplification to obtain (2m-1)^2.",
    "sentence-category": "Implement"
  },
  {
    "index": 22,
    "sentence": "So we need $mn-1$ to divide $(2m-1)^2$.",
    "sentence-type": "answer",
    "sentence-category-reason": "Draws the immediate divisibility requirement from the previous computation.",
    "sentence-category": "Implement"
  },
  {
    "index": 23,
    "sentence": "Similarly, we need $mn-1$ to divide $(2n-1)^2$.",
